WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.970
The long-awaited sequel to Guardians of the
Galaxy revisited everything fans loved about

00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:09.470
the first film, while moving the story forward
in a wild and imaginative way — and critics

00:00:09.470 --> 00:00:13.390
and audiences alike agree that it was one
of Marvel's most fun movies to date.

00:00:13.390 --> 00:00:18.740
And yet, even in an altogether terrific followup,
there are still some weird plot holes, quirky

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:22.250
easter eggs, and continuity questions that
left us scratching our heads while the end

00:00:22.250 --> 00:00:23.250
credits rolled.

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:27.310
Yeah, it's all small potatoes compared to
the movie's winning qualities, but here are

00:00:27.310 --> 00:00:31.760
all the dumb things from Guardians of the
Galaxy Vol. 2 that left us feeling a little...

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:32.920
unsettled.

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:37.050
Star Lord's tracking device

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:41.500
From his outfit to his entertainment system,
Star-Lord is all about the eighties — including

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:44.350
when it comes to the tech he whips out in
this opening scene.

00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:48.950
This vintage Mattel football video game has
been rewired to work as a tracking device,

00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:50.420
but where did it come from?

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:54.660
There are only two options: one, that Quill
stumbled upon it during his travels around

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:58.400
the galaxy, which would be a weird coincidence
to say the least.

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:01.920
Or two, maybe he had it on him when he was
kidnapped from the earth as a kid — but

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:05.870
unlike his Walkman, he wasn't too sentimental
about this toy to repurpose it into a useful

00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:06.870
piece of tech.

00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:10.640
Why are the Guardians doing the Sovereigns'
dirty work?

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:15.619
This big action set piece pitting the Guardians
against a space monster known as an Abelisk

00:01:15.619 --> 00:01:19.969
was such an amazing scene, you can almost
ignore the obvious question: why were the

00:01:19.969 --> 00:01:21.720
Guardians there in the first place?

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:23.490
The Sovereign leader offers a reason —

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:27.899
"We could not risk the lives of our own Sovereign
citizens."

00:01:27.899 --> 00:01:33.180
— but they wouldn't have to; as we find
out shortly, they own an entire fleet of remote-controlled

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:36.159
ships that seem perfectly equipped for a job
like this.

00:01:36.159 --> 00:01:40.280
What prompted them to punt this job to a shady
group of misfits like the Guardians?

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:42.200
We may never know.

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:44.459
Ego's relationship with Mantis

00:01:44.459 --> 00:01:48.369
Director James Gunn wanted his sequel to include
some characters from the comics that didn't

00:01:48.369 --> 00:01:52.670
make the cut the first time around, and Mantis,
who has a long and storied history with the

00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:54.289
Guardians, was an obvious choice.

00:01:54.289 --> 00:01:59.680
But to fit her in, Gunn had to tweak her story
— so in the movie, she's basically an indentured

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:04.439
adopted daughter to Ego, using her empathic
powers to help him sleep at night.

00:02:04.439 --> 00:02:05.439
Wait, what?

00:02:05.439 --> 00:02:09.959
Ego is an ancient, powerful celestial being
— and he needs an empath to sleep at night?

00:02:09.959 --> 00:02:14.349
How did he sleep in the thousands and thousands
of years before Mantis came along?

00:02:14.349 --> 00:02:19.320
And why would he let her, the only person
who knew his true intentions, hang out unchaperoned

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:22.519
with the Guardians where she could spill the
beans at any time?

00:02:22.519 --> 00:02:23.519
Well, whatever.

00:02:23.519 --> 00:02:28.040
Maybe Ego can't just manifest a supply of
Ambien the way he manifested an entire planet,

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:32.120
and maybe his god-like status made him a little
overconfident about his nefarious plan.

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:36.120
But most importantly, maybe we're so psyched
to have Mantis in this movie that her backstory

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:39.069
doesn't really need to hold up to scrutiny.

00:02:39.069 --> 00:02:41.120
Who's your Hasselhoff?

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:45.930
In this film, we learn that Quill keeps a
pic of Knight Rider-era David Hasselhoff in

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:50.250
his pocket, because as a fatherless kid, he
used to tell people the Hoff was his real

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:56.000
dad — a subplot that leads to this crowd-pleasing
cameo from Hasselhoff as one of Ego's various

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:57.000
avatars.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:01.969
There's just one problem: this Hoff is a senior
citizen, whereas Quill's aspirational dad

00:03:01.969 --> 00:03:04.390
was the strapping young Hoff of the 1980s.

00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:09.450
In the context of the story, and for maximum
emotional impact on Quill, Ego should have

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:11.920
appeared as that version of Hasselhoff.

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:15.159
But if that was going to make the difference
between working with the Hoff directly versus

00:03:15.159 --> 00:03:19.400
recreating his younger self using CGI, it's
not hard to see why the filmmakers preferred

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:20.840
the real thing.

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:23.340
The truth about Quill's mom

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:27.940
Ego is still making a hard pitch for Quill
to join him when he also spills a horrifying

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:32.631
secret: he's the one who gave Quill's mom
cancer, a fact he reveals to his son while

00:03:32.631 --> 00:03:35.379
also trying to woo him over to the dark side.

00:03:35.379 --> 00:03:39.430
But for some strange reason, learning that
Ego is responsible for the worst trauma of

00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:43.770
his life doesn't make Quill feel like teaming
up with him — an outcome so predictable

00:03:43.770 --> 00:03:47.129
that any celestial being worth his salt should've
seen it coming.

00:03:47.129 --> 00:03:51.829
And equally inexplicable is why Ego bothered
to off Quill's mother at all.

00:03:51.829 --> 00:03:52.829
What gives, dude?

00:03:52.829 --> 00:03:56.060
"I returned to earth to see her three times.

00:03:56.060 --> 00:03:59.870
And I knew, if I returned a fourth, I'd never
leave."

00:03:59.870 --> 00:04:02.459
That's his story, but we don't buy it.

00:04:02.459 --> 00:04:05.739
Everything we know about Ego suggests he's
a raging sociopath who doesn't care about

00:04:05.739 --> 00:04:10.500
anyone — and since he planned all along
to kidnap Quill from earth, taking mom out

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:12.420
of the equation seems pointless.

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:16.989
Plus, Ego has been alive for millions of years,
so what's the big rush?

00:04:16.989 --> 00:04:21.310
Why not just be happy for 40 or 50 years and
then complete the masterplan once she dies

00:04:21.310 --> 00:04:22.310
of old age or something?

00:04:22.310 --> 00:04:27.090
Of course, this is still an important moment
in terms of establishing Ego as a cut-and-dried

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:31.810
bad guy and giving Quill a good reason to
want to destroy him, but it's one of a handful

00:04:31.810 --> 00:04:34.430
that doesn't make narrative sense.

00:04:34.430 --> 00:04:36.590
No continuity for old Marvel

00:04:36.590 --> 00:04:41.460
The timeline of the Marvel Cinematic Universe
has always been kind of fuzzy, but Guardians

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:46.490
of the Galaxy Volume 2 represents a real missed
opportunity for MCU continuity.

00:04:46.490 --> 00:04:50.580
Here's the deal: Ego's attempt to take control
of the earth occurs around the same time as

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:55.389
films like Captain America: Civil War and
the ABC series Agents of S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:04:55.389 --> 00:05:00.530
Surely the MCU's top crimefighters would have
noticed the giant tide of alien goo engulfing

00:05:00.530 --> 00:05:05.090
half of Missouri — and a crossover cameo
in either direction would have really helped

00:05:05.090 --> 00:05:06.710
to tie this universe together.

00:05:06.710 --> 00:05:11.949
Sadly, Ego's incursion on earth never earned
so much as a nod in these other Marvel properties,

00:05:11.949 --> 00:05:18.160
and in Guardians 2, no other MCU characters
ever showed up to investigate the goo situation.

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:20.440
The invincible Milano

00:05:20.440 --> 00:05:23.220
We all know that Star-Lord has a very cool
ship.

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:27.949
The Milano is fast, slick, comes loaded with
a tape deck for all your soundtrack needs,

00:05:27.949 --> 00:05:29.389
and makes a hell of a mobile man-cave.

00:05:29.389 --> 00:05:35.039
"If I had a blacklight, this place would look
like a Jackson Pollock painting."

00:05:35.039 --> 00:05:37.349
But is the Milano literally indestructible?

00:05:37.349 --> 00:05:42.430
The ship takes a pounding during this battle
with the Sovereign's forces, in which dozens

00:05:42.430 --> 00:05:45.730
and dozens of ships fire point blank on the
Guardians.

00:05:45.730 --> 00:05:50.949
Yet Star-Lord's ship weathers all that damage,
when other ships have gone down with just

00:05:50.949 --> 00:05:51.949
a shot or two.

00:05:51.949 --> 00:05:56.879
How does the Milano survive this veritable
onslaught of laser fire while still maintaining

00:05:56.879 --> 00:05:59.520
enough integrity to stumble toward a crash
landing?

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:04.240
In this case, the answer is simple: plot armor
— the same reason why Stormtroopers have

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:08.850
such terrible aim and James Bond can somehow
win a fistfight with a henchman twice his

00:06:08.850 --> 00:06:09.850
size.

00:06:09.850 --> 00:06:14.020
If one direct hit could knock the hero's ship
out of the sky, there would be no movie.

00:06:14.020 --> 00:06:15.020
Thanks for watching!

00:06:15.020 --> 00:06:17.350
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:17.350 --> 00:06:20.210
And don't forget to check out all this cool
stuff we know you'll love too.

